   "source": [
    "## 📝 Knowledge Check\n",
    "\n",
    "**Instructions:** Answer the following questions in this markdown cell.\n",
    "\n",
    "1.  **In your own words, what is the key difference between a regression problem and a classification problem?**\n",
    "2. **The `LinearRegression` model has an attribute called `.coef_`. After you train the model, print `lr_model.coef_`. What do these numbers represent?**\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Why did we use `mean_squared_error` to evaluate the regression model but `accuracy_score` for the classification model?** Why wouldn't accuracy be a good metric for the fare prediction task?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Answers:**\n",
    "\n",
    "1.) A regression problem predicts a number, such as Fare or Age, whereas a classification problem predicts a category, such as Pclass, Survived, or Embarked.\n",
    "\n",
    "2.) The coefficients are the slopes: how much the predicted fare changes when each feature goes up by 1. The first number (−0.44) is Age's slope, meaning for every one year older someone is, the model lowers the fare guess by about 44 cents, if their class stays the same. In plain terms, age had little impact on fare. Class had a much bigger effect: the fare drops roughly \\$38 for each step down in class (Pclass going from 1 to 2, or 2 to 3)(−38.30).\n",
    "\n",
    "3.) Regression predicts numbers, like Fare, so the model almost never guesses the exact answer. If the real fare was \$14.45 and the model guessed \$14.50, accuracy would count that as wrong. That's why we use MSE: it shows how far off the model was. Accuracy only works for classification, like survived yes or no, where the model either got it right or it didn't, so we just count how many times it was right.\n"
   ]
  }
